KEYWORDS: MOF nanocrystals, gas separations, mixed-matrix membranes, phase-change MOFs, \(C O_{2}/N_{2}\) separations
In the coming decades, climate change will likely lead to environmental and economic disruption.1 Carbon dioxide, the primary greenhouse gas produced from the combustion of fossil fuels, is currently released into the atmosphere without mitigation at a rate of 36 Gton per year.2 Carbon capture and sequestration has been proposed to reduce the amount of atmospheric \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . However, implementation of this technology necessitates low-cost and efficient methods for separating \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) in a postcombustion capture process, where the carbon dioxide is removed from the effluent of power plants that burn coal or natural gas.3 One of the most energy-efficient ways of capturing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is with a membrane-based separation.4 Among the design platforms available for gas separation membranes, mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs) are promising: MMMs offer the low cost, high processability, and mechanical properties of polymers and, if successful, the unbounded separation performance of porous materials. Many MMMs overcome the intrinsic permeability-selectivity trade-off of pure polymers.5,6 In recent years, zeolites and metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have been used as fillers in MMMs7−9 where typically molecular sieving and solution-diffusion mechanisms10 promote gas separation. In this work, we introduce for the first time a phase-change MOF,11 mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) (mmen \(=\ N,N^{\prime}\) -dimethylethylenediamine; dobpd \(\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\hat{~}{~4,4^{\prime}}~}~}\) -
dioxidobiphenyl- \(^{3,3^{\prime}}\) -dicarboxylate, in a MMM to enhance the permeability and selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (Figure 1).
While the use of mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) as a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorbent and its associated thermodynamic underpinnings have been investigated thoroughly,11−16 the kinetics of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) transport in this phase-change material in a driven (i.e., out-of-equilibrium) system have not yet been reported; thus, their prospects for improving MMM-based gas separations are still unclear. It has been demonstrated that mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) has a novel cooperative \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake mechanism that leads to a nonclassical, steplike adsorption isotherm.11 Having such a high, readily reversible \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake makes the application of mmen\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) in membranes very attractive. Until now, attempts to incorporate this phase-change MOF in an MMM have failed, likely due to the dimensions of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) crystals obtained through conventional synthetic approaches; as shown in Figure S1, high aspect-ratio microrods, when blended with polymer, typically protrude from the matrix, which also appear to exhibit poorly formed polymer/MOF interfaces. To resolve issues associated with compositing larger MOF crystals,
Figure 1. By incorporating phase-change MOFs in a mixed-matrix membrane, the permeability and selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separations can be greatly enhanced. Critical to the design of these composite membranes is the nanoscale dimension of the phase-change MOF filler, which serves to mitigate the effects of plasticization and likelihood of defects in the membrane that would negatively impact gas transport selectivity. Critical to the performance of the membranes is the dynamic uptake and release of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), along the diamine molecules appended to open metal sites lining the MOF channels.
we developed a new synthesis of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals. In turn, diamines were grafted within these \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals to take advantage of their steplike \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorptive properties in an MMM for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separations. With these new diamine-appended \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals incorporated into MMMs, we find significant increases in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability while maintaining high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity relative to the neat polymer matrix, essentially uncoupling conventional permeability-selectivity trade-offs typified by all-polymer membranes. This result points to new opportunities for MOF nanocrystals to enhance the performance of MMMs\(^{9,17}\) and
encourages further investigation of other phase-change adsorbents as active components in gas-separation membranes. To access \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals for MMM preparation, we developed a new synthetic procedure with the goal of obtaining dispersible nanocrystals with properties similar to bulk crystals (Figure 2). We were ultimately successful by introducing the \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) ligand to a superstoichiometric amount of \(\mathrm{MgO}\) in DMF at \(120\ ^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},^{18}\) which generates a coating of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods on \(\mathrm{MgO}\). Precursor stoichiometry and reaction time were varied in turn to obtain \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods with controlled morphology and length (e.g., \(100{-}200\ \mathrm{~nm}\) in size) (Figure 2). To isolate
MOF nanorods, the \(\mathrm{MgO}\) core was simply dissolved in acetic acid. The resulting purified MOF nanorods (Figure S3) were found to unaltered by the acetic acid treatment and they could be dispersed readily in polar aprotic solvents, such as DMF. Visualization of the reaction pathway is depicted in Figure S2. Notably, we found that higher Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface areas (Figure S4a) and higher amine loadings (Figure S4b), were achieved after treating the nanorods in a subsequent step with \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) in DMF at \(120\ \ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) consistent with the presence of fewer defects in the final material.
Differences in the crystallinity of the \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods compared to the micron-scale rods obtained from a bulk preparation are evident in Figure 3. Both experimental diffraction patterns match the calculated reflections for the crystal structure, and the peak broadening observed for the nano-MOF sample is consistent with the expected Scherrer broadening. Using Scherrer analysis, we estimate the crystallite size to be \({\sim}12\ \mathrm{\nm},\) corresponding to the average nanorod cross-section. We further characterized the BET surface area for both samples (Figure S5), which unexpectedly revealed a greater surface area for the \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods than for the
microrods. Deviations in behavior with regard to the shape of the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherm manifests at high pressures, likely due to adsorption at surface sites, which are more prevalent in the nano-MOF.
To complete the preparation of the phase-change filler, \(N,N^{\prime}\) - dimethylethylenediamine (mmen) molecules were grafted to the Lewis acidic open-metal sites within \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) in toluene. No activation procedure was employed prior to diamine grafting, as we found drying to be detrimental to achieving good nanorod dispersions in the polymer matrix. After activating both nano- and micron-scale samples of mmen\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) MOFs at \(100\ \ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) their \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake was evaluated. The resulting adsorption isotherms are shown in Figure 3 as linear and semilog plots. The linear plot (Figure 3b) indicates \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) saturation capacity is quite comparable for the two crystal sizes, while the semilog plot (Figure 3c) reveals that the adsorption "step" of the mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods is broader, corresponding to a lesser degree of cooperativity in the adsorption mechanism.19 This could be due to finite-size effects in the formation of carbamate chains that are limited by the nanocrystal length or it could be the consequence of a higher number of mmen vacancies within the crystal. To put an upper
bound on the latter, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out on nano- and micron-sized MOFs to quantify the mmen loadings. The results indicate a decrease in mmen loading within the nanorods of only \(1.4\%\) (Figure S6), which we believe to be negligible (i.e., within the experimental error).
To understand the influence of phase-change MOF fillers on membrane performance, we cast MMMs from a mixture of 6FDA-DAM polyimide and mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) nanorods in dichloromethane. The quality of the dispersion was excellent, yielding transparent, flexible membranes after solvent evaporation. These membranes were tested in a custom-built, constant-volume, variable-pressure, gas-permeation apparatus. Permeabilities of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) were recorded and selectivities were calculated from the acquired data at different input (upstream) pressures. Both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability (Figure 4a) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity (Figure 4b) are greater in the MMM than in the neat polymer. To better clarify the role of the diamines inside the MOF, we prepared a membrane with bare \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods within 6FDA-DAM and tested \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) permeation under the same conditions. The results, plotted in Figure 4c, are consistent with previous reports that showed that nano \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) /polymer MMMs outperform pure polymer membranes.20 Figure 4c also shows that the diamines in mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) nanorods significantly improve \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability while also positively affecting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity (Table S1).
We further performed gas permeation measurements on both 6FDA-DAM and mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc)−6FDA-DAM membranes (Figure S7) operating at higher temperatures \((35{-}75\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . We noted that the decrease in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity with increasing temperature for the 6FDA-DAM membrane was significantly less pronounced than that for the mmen\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc}){-}6\mathrm{FDA-DAM}\) MMM. We expect that with increasing temperature, solubility and selectivity are reduced in both the neat polymer and in the MMM, with solubility playing a more pronounced role in the adsorbent-loaded material. Considering the temperature-dependent steplike character of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and release that is the hallmark characteristic of the phase-change mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) filler, adsorption sites within the MOF are left increasingly unoccupied at higher temperature, effectively decreasing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) solubility of the MMM. This effect dominates the steep decrease in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity with increasing temperature in the MMM and corroborates our hypothesis that amineappended MOFs primarily improve the performance of MMMs by increasing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) solubility. These data also suggest that the prevalence of interfacial polymer−MOF voids in the MMM is minimal or at least inconsequential.
On the basis of these results, we conclude that the diamines have an important role in the increase of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability and that this is likely related to the unique \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) cooperative adsorption behavior. We further speculate that selectivities could increase in real operating conditions, for example, in a mixed-gas environment. Indeed, under pure \(\mathrm{N}_{2},\) the transport is unaffected by \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the pore and we may expect higher \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) permeability. Under a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) environment, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) can still transport down the channel while \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) may be excluded due to the carbamate formation that effectively reduce the pore volume. We note that in absolute terms other polymers (e.g., polymers of intrinsic microporosity or thermally-rearranged polymers) can achieve superior performance in terms of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability, although their lack of stability over time has been a serious concern, preventing their use in industrial
applications.21,22 MMMs are generally less susceptible to aging, and MOF nanocrystal−polymer composite membranes are resistant to plasticization.17 Moreover, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption is not affected by humidity in the amine-appended adsorbents,21 unlike what is observed in metal−organic frameworks with bare metal sites.22,23
The presence of diamines in these phase-change MOF fillers could influence \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) transport through several mechanisms, and the results presented here lend some insight into the relative impact of each. With diamines present in the MOF channel, the free diffusion of both gases is restricted due to the decrease in free volume relative to the bare framework. On the other hand, with diamines present \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is chemisorbed strongly and selectively through the cooperative formation of ammonium carbamate chains; weak physisorption of either gas is still possible. Comparing the pressures used in the permeability measurements to the room-temperature \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm for a sample of mmen- \(\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\) (dobpdc) nanorods suggests that ammonium carbamate formation should be highly favored throughout the membrane, since even the lower downstream pressure rapidly exceeds the 1 mbar adsorption threshold during the measurement. Because \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) saturation is measured at equilibrium, an increase in solubility, rather than diffusivity, more likely explains the increase in permeability. A detectable increase in solubility during steady-state permeability measurements suggests fast exchange between carbamate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and mobile \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the pores. To our knowledge, the kinetics of this exchange is not yet well understood, and our results thus motivate further study of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) dynamics in this system. A classical model of facilitated transport25 captures some aspects of this system, but does not adequately describe the cooperative uptake. As \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) does not participate in cooperative adsorption, its permeability is less dependent on the MOF chemical functionality and generally trends with free volume, which should be higher in the MMMs. Interestingly, the modest increase in selectivity upon the addition of diamines may be related to diamine pore-blocking impeding \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) transport.
As further theoretical studies aimed at understanding out-of-equilibrium kinetics of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption along the diamines emerge, our work presents the first validated guidepost as to their significance in phase-change MMM design rules. Our insights in that regard benefitted from pinhole free composites, which were only available when the particle dimensions were scaled to nanoscopic dimensions, introducing along the way a novel synthesis of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods based on kinetic control over the reaction coordinate. Our phase-change MMMs, when tested for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separation, yielded a 2-fold improvement in the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability, as well as a similarly high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity, boding well for other membrane-based gas separations where the selector incorporates phase-change fillers.
